  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nDry weather will continue through at least early Sunday before \nan unsettled weather pattern unfolds Sunday through Monday \nnight.\n\nA ridge will be in place to the west of the local area through\nMonday with a trough setting up across the northeastern CONUS. \nA series of disturbances will crest the ridge Sunday through \nMonday night, resulting in multiple chances of showers and \nthunderstorms through the start of the week. There's still quite\na bit of uncertainty in the timing/placement of thunderstorms \nand the resulting severe weather threat across the local area. \nThunderstorm chances and the trajectories of any MCS activity \nwill rely on mesoscale features that have yet to be resolved. \n\nSome CAMs suggest that scattered diurnal showers and thunderstorms \ncould develop southeast of Lake Erie Sunday afternoon. There may be \nan isolated severe weather threat during that time given the \nincreasing moisture and instability across the area, but the\nthreat is pretty isolated at this point. Meanwhile, an MCS will\nfollow an instability gradient and push south/southeast from \nthe Upper Midwest during peak heating before either clipping or \nmissing the local area just to the west Sunday evening or \novernight. Will need to continue to monitor CAMs and adjust the \nforecast as needed; as of now confidence in severe weather \npotential Sunday is low and the threat is conditional. There is \na Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 5) of severe weather across most\nlocal Ohio zones.\n\nConfidence in thunderstorms is higher for Monday since the ridge \nwill be shifting east into a more favorable position for \nconvection making it into the area. There may be periodic \nshowers/thunderstorms throughout the day Monday, although \nthunderstorm chances will rely on how Sunday night/early Monday \nmorning pans out. Another MCS will likely push southeast or \ndevelop along remnant boundaries across the region at some point\nlate in the day Monday into Monday night. Deep layer shear will\nbe higher on Monday thanks to better upper level support as a\ncold front approaches so there is a Slight Risk (Level 2 of 5) \nof severe weather across the entire area Monday/Monday night. \nDamaging wind gusts will most likely be the main threat. Will \ncontinue to keep an eye on model trends and update the \nforecast/messaging as confidence in timing and coverage \nincreases.\n\nShower and thunderstorm chances will continue into Tuesday with lake-\nenhanced rain showers/thunderstorms possibly continuing across \nNE OH/NW PA into Wednesday due to persistent troughing overhead.\nDry weather will return by early Thursday as high pressure\nbriefly builds in from the west.",
